[COMMENTS]
STATION,Rated station gauging a relatively urban catchment near Stoke-on-Trent. GENERAL: Time-of-flight ultrasonic installed at the site, however, peak flow data are from ratings. STATION HYDROMETRY: Multipath ultrasonic gauge installed beneath A34 road bridge on a gentle curve. Ultrasonic gauge replaced with a new NivuFlow time of flight setup in February 2022. However, peak flow data are from three peak flow ratings applied across period of record, derived for flood forecasting. Out-of-bank flows inferred by the rated flow from spot gaugings. Site experiences large silt deposits on left bank due to upstream bend in the river. This can affect ultrasonic performance though is managed. High flow ADCP gaugings carried out using a rope from the road bridge. Low flow gaugings carried out in the ultrasonic section using remote controlled ADCP. FLOW RECORD: AMAX levels in water years 1985/86 and 1986/87 are suspect, but AMAX and daily mean flow magnitudes are consistent with analogue station 28040 upstream. Station undermined in 1987 flood, reconstructed 1990/1. In 2001-2002, gauge not performing v well. Transducers failing constantly, especially lower ones (due to silt). Problems in 2002-2003 with rubble in channel now creating skew flows & siltation. Full period of record peak flow data reviewed and released in August 2025 (WINFAP Files v14). FLOW REGIME: Quite responsive but flow regime dominated by Strongford WRW discharge (Stoke-on-Trent).
CATCHMENT,Moderate relief catchment, substantially urbanised with Stoke-on-Trent and Newcastle-under-Lyme. Geology mainly Coal Measures and Marls, Millstone Grit and subordinate Sherwood S'st, widely covered by Boulder Clay. Mining, industrial and mixed agricultural land use.
Qmed Suitability,Well gauged to QMED, albeit with a lot of scatter.
Pooling Suitability,Uncertainty caused by scatter in gaugings limits suitability for Pooling, despite gaugings above QMED fitting the rating well.
[END]
